>I think it'd be nice to have the skinny on what having
>"half-resistance" means in game terms.

I thought it was obvious, but I must've been wrong. Okay! Full
resistance prevents all of the damage from occurring, while half
resistance only prevents half the damage from occurring.

>Is it possible up upgrade half resistance into full, maybe by getting
>it from two sources?

Half resistance doesn't stack. My rule of thumb is: you cannot get
permanent _intrinsic_ full resistance. (Note the "permanent" - this is
opening the door for a possible upgrade where I add the "ward against
flame/ice" spells from Slash'EM which provide temporary full
resistance)

>This makes magic resistance a lesser resistance, and spell resistance
>becomes what magic resistance was.

You're forgetting something important, my friend: Angels.

>I base this statement on the fact that, in vanilla Nethack, while magic
>resistance is useful for many things, the thing that makes it necessary
>to any non-challenge ascension kit, instead of just greatly
>recommended, is that it protects against death rays, touch of death and
>Death's actual touch

If you apply this patch in conjunction with my Monster Spells patch,
the game-breakingly uncool Touch of Death is rightfully vanished.
As for death rays, there's always reflection or polymorph.

Though, for Death herself, I might as well be proactive and in the next
version remove her attack's one-hit-kill aspect - or, more likely, the
ability of Spell Resistance to counteract it. (C'mon, it's Death! She
deserves a random unblockable one-hit-kill!)

>This makes this problem *worse*: using GDSM to
>avoid touch of death no longer works, and you can't use quest artifacts
>for it either!

One: most r.g.r.n members feel that quest artifact magic resistance is
too easily vorbed by the Wizard's theft move, and two, I added the
Amulet Versus Spells to balance out the lack of functionality of gray
dragon scales.

>Why?  What's wrong with Barbarians having "full" poison resistance?

See my rule of thumb. Elves/sleep resistance and orcs/poison resistance
are the only exceptions, owing to their source mythos.

>Does half fire resistance protect against max HP loss?  Remember that
>Genhennom is full of fire traps, which is why fire resistance is
>recommended before going there.

Actually, no it doesn't. So you'd better invest in rings of fire
resistance or red dragon scale mail!

>What about the wide-angle disintegration beam?

Yeah, it's wide _angle_ - that means it never misses. It still only
hits once.



